  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 PM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nDRY THROUGH THURSDAY: \n\nHigh pressure building to our south will support a shift from\nsouthwesterly to more west/northwesterly flow by tomorrow. The \nshift to more zonal flow will bring drier air to the region. \nThe drier air will remain in place through Thursday, which \nmakes precipitation unlikely until late in the week. Although, a\nfew light showers are possible over the San Juans and central \nDivide this afternoon, but most of this convective activity is \nexpected to favor the eastern slopes of these high terrain \nareas. Temperatures around 5 to 10 degrees above normal are \nforecast over the next few days.\n\nThe drier air will result in relative humidities falling below\nfire weather thresholds during the late afternoon and early\nevening, particularly tomorrow and Thursday. However, wind \ngusts will only be marginal at most, resulting in just \nlocalized critical fire weather conditions in the Four Corners \nregion and some areas along the Divide. \n\nPOSSIBLE MOISTURE RETURN LATE IN THE WEEK:\n\nCurrent guidance favors modest moisture advection bringing\nprecipitable water anomalies around 125% to 175% of normal \nlater this week. This moisture advection is reliant on the \ndevelopment of a trough in the Pacific Northwest and a resultant\nshift in flow over our area. There is good agreement between \ndeterministic models on the development of this trough. However,\nthe reliance on the upstream trough and the absence of any \nobvious forcing on the synoptic level keeps confidence low \nregarding the specifics of our potential moisture push. So, be \nsure to stay tuned to see how the weekend forecast develops \nover the next few days.",
